  "style": {
    "id": "guilloche",
    "name": "Guilloche",
    "description": "Two dials, endless lace: a silver dial on a small brass rose engine on a dark-teal bench, seen close. A fixed steel graver on a brass slide touches the turning disc and cuts one wavy ring per turn, r(θ) = R_k + a·sin(n·θ + k·s); ring after ring, each shifted a little, the waves weave a rosette. The grooves are not geometry: one shader on the disc evaluates the nearby rings in closed form for every pixel, gives each a V cross-section and tilts the normal, so the cut walls glint under a procedural studio environment as the disc turns. Two brass dials set the lobe count n and the phase step s; turning one re-forms every ring at once. Finally translucent deep-blue enamel floods across and a light sweeps over the finished dial. Lathe hum, a rising cutting hiss per ring, ratchet clicks per dial step, a soft pour and a bell.",
